=== HINT 1: Convert + create ===
g_scheduler_fiber = ConvertThreadToFiber(NULL);
for (int i = 0; i < NUM_FIBERS; i++)
    g_fibers[i] = CreateFiber(0, fiber_fn, &ctxs[i]);

=== HINT 2: Yield + dispatch ===
In fiber_fn: SwitchToFiber(g_scheduler_fiber);   // suspend me, resume scheduler
In the scheduler loop, inside if (!g_done[i]):
    SwitchToFiber(g_fibers[i]);                  // returns when the fiber yields

=== HINT 3 (KEY INSIGHT) ===
SwitchToFiber never enters the kernel - it is a user-mode context switch
(registers, stack pointer, FPU state), roughly 50-100 ns vs ~1000 ns for a
thread switch. Fibers are single-threaded cooperative: only one runs at a
time per OS thread - the foundation of green threads / coroutine schedulers.
